1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,975
In this video, I'm going to
talk about Git workflows.

2
00:00:03,975 --> 00:00:07,685
I want to introduce the Git
Feature Branch Workflow

3
00:00:07,685 --> 00:00:11,320
and also introduce
what's called GitFlow.

4
00:00:11,690 --> 00:00:14,640
The Git feature
branch workflow is

5
00:00:14,640 --> 00:00:18,330
just a way that you can

6
00:00:18,330 --> 00:00:20,460
use Git such that all

7
00:00:20,460 --> 00:00:22,290
your feature development would

8
00:00:22,290 --> 00:00:24,330
take place on a
dedicated branch.

9
00:00:24,330 --> 00:00:27,435
You would use the master
branch, not for development,

10
00:00:27,435 --> 00:00:30,390
but just for tracking

11
00:00:30,390 --> 00:00:33,910
every time a specific
feature is completed.

12
00:00:34,220 --> 00:00:37,010
The nice thing about this
implementation is that

13
00:00:37,010 --> 00:00:38,870
you can have multiple
developers that are

14
00:00:38,870 --> 00:00:41,270
working on specific
or different features

15
00:00:41,270 --> 00:00:43,750
without disturbing
that main codebase.

16
00:00:43,750 --> 00:00:46,265
You can have these developers

17
00:00:46,265 --> 00:00:49,100
working on same feature
branch, for instance,

18
00:00:49,100 --> 00:00:50,660
and sharing that feature branch,

19
00:00:50,660 --> 00:00:53,090
but not breaking anything

20
00:00:53,090 --> 00:00:55,285
that's working in
the master branch.

21
00:00:55,285 --> 00:00:57,140
A key concept here is that

22
00:00:57,140 --> 00:00:58,820
the master branch
is never broken.

23
00:00:58,820 --> 00:01:01,325
You don't actually
merge in any changes to

24
00:01:01,325 --> 00:01:04,190
your development branches until

25
00:01:04,190 --> 00:01:07,400
you complete and tested
with that change,

26
00:01:07,400 --> 00:01:11,705
and you've had some level
of verification that you

27
00:01:11,705 --> 00:01:13,600
aren't going to break anything

28
00:01:13,600 --> 00:01:16,805
that's already working
in the master branch.

29
00:01:16,805 --> 00:01:18,650
The feature will end with

30
00:01:18,650 --> 00:01:20,330
a pull request or
a merge request

31
00:01:20,330 --> 00:01:21,575
from the feature branch,

32
00:01:21,575 --> 00:01:24,170
and the pull or merge request is

33
00:01:24,170 --> 00:01:26,970
a great opportunity to
put in code reviews.

34
00:01:26,970 --> 00:01:29,030
Other people on the
team who maybe weren't

35
00:01:29,030 --> 00:01:31,040
as involved with development can

36
00:01:31,040 --> 00:01:33,800
review the work that you
did and ask questions

37
00:01:33,800 --> 00:01:37,920
or ask for changes
based on what they see.

38
00:01:38,540 --> 00:01:41,480
An example of how you'd use

39
00:01:41,480 --> 00:01:43,790
Git for feature branch workflow.

40
00:01:43,790 --> 00:01:45,785
You'd start by checking
out the master.

41
00:01:45,785 --> 00:01:48,020
You would pull origin master,

42
00:01:48,020 --> 00:01:49,400
you'd make sure you are in sync

43
00:01:49,400 --> 00:01:51,355
with the origin master branch.

44
00:01:51,355 --> 00:01:53,460
Then you can do git checkout -b

45
00:01:53,460 --> 00:01:55,305
for your feature branch name.

46
00:01:55,305 --> 00:01:57,710
That feature branch
name is often something

47
00:01:57,710 --> 00:02:00,440
like issue-25 or so.

48
00:02:00,440 --> 00:02:03,080
For instance, if you're
using GitHub issue tracking,

49
00:02:03,080 --> 00:02:06,200
or it could be something
like feature/foo,

50
00:02:06,200 --> 00:02:08,525
where foo describes the
name of the feature,

51
00:02:08,525 --> 00:02:12,690
or maybe it's fix-foo
if you're fixing a bug.

52
00:02:14,260 --> 00:02:16,430
My personal preference of these,

53
00:02:16,430 --> 00:02:18,245
is something like issue-25.

54
00:02:18,245 --> 00:02:20,870
The reason is if you

55
00:02:20,870 --> 00:02:24,200
use some words to describe
your feature or your fix,

56
00:02:24,200 --> 00:02:25,460
it might not be obvious to

57
00:02:25,460 --> 00:02:29,220
other people what
exactly you're fixing.

58
00:02:29,220 --> 00:02:31,970
Whereas if you link back to
an issue tracking database,

59
00:02:31,970 --> 00:02:33,650
you can put all the detail
that you want about

60
00:02:33,650 --> 00:02:35,600
that and you can
update that easily

61
00:02:35,600 --> 00:02:37,700
to include additional
description

62
00:02:37,700 --> 00:02:40,630
about what you're
changing or implementing.

63
00:02:40,630 --> 00:02:42,410
But your development
team will have

64
00:02:42,410 --> 00:02:43,940
its own guidelines about how

65
00:02:43,940 --> 00:02:45,410
to do this and you should follow

66
00:02:45,410 --> 00:02:47,680
whatever your team is
suggesting to use.

67
00:02:47,680 --> 00:02:50,105
But you'll ultimately
check out a branch,

68
00:02:50,105 --> 00:02:52,175
do all your commits
on that branch.

69
00:02:52,175 --> 00:02:54,080
Then when you're
ready to share with

70
00:02:54,080 --> 00:02:56,375
others or when you're
ready to do a code review,

71
00:02:56,375 --> 00:02:57,740
you would do git push origin

72
00:02:57,740 --> 00:03:00,060
with your feature branch name.

73
00:03:00,250 --> 00:03:03,110
At that point, you'll
be ready to do

74
00:03:03,110 --> 00:03:06,620
a code review on
the feature branch.

75
00:03:06,620 --> 00:03:08,390
This would typically
be assigned to

76
00:03:08,390 --> 00:03:11,450
some other person on the team
that would add comments,

77
00:03:11,450 --> 00:03:13,370
questions, or suggestions based

78
00:03:13,370 --> 00:03:15,940
on the code that you
put in the branch.

79
00:03:15,940 --> 00:03:17,510
It will go back to
the developer then to

80
00:03:17,510 --> 00:03:19,295
respond to those questions,

81
00:03:19,295 --> 00:03:22,890
make new changes based on
the pull request comments.

82
00:03:22,900 --> 00:03:24,950
At that point, you can merge

83
00:03:24,950 --> 00:03:27,490
the feature into
the master branch.

84
00:03:27,490 --> 00:03:29,240
Once you've verified
that it works,

85
00:03:29,240 --> 00:03:31,640
that you haven't broken
anything in the master branch,

86
00:03:31,640 --> 00:03:33,980
you could merge
these changes here

87
00:03:33,980 --> 00:03:36,290
in purple that were done
on your development branch

88
00:03:36,290 --> 00:03:38,180
back into the master
branch and have

89
00:03:38,180 --> 00:03:39,860
the equivalent
functionality here

90
00:03:39,860 --> 00:03:41,195
now in the master branch.

91
00:03:41,195 --> 00:03:42,770
But one thing that
you might notice is

92
00:03:42,770 --> 00:03:44,660
this extra little
white dot here,

93
00:03:44,660 --> 00:03:46,955
meaning that after
we did our branch,

94
00:03:46,955 --> 00:03:49,965
there's another change
here on the master.

95
00:03:49,965 --> 00:03:51,915
Why would this
commit be present?

96
00:03:51,915 --> 00:03:53,400
Well, it'd be present

97
00:03:53,400 --> 00:03:55,670
because some other
feature was merged in

98
00:03:55,670 --> 00:03:58,070
after we started our development

99
00:03:58,070 --> 00:04:00,010
on this development branch.

100
00:04:00,010 --> 00:04:01,910
How do we handle this scenario

101
00:04:01,910 --> 00:04:04,370
that there was a change
on the master branch?

102
00:04:04,370 --> 00:04:07,625
Git will take care of this
for us with a merge commit.

103
00:04:07,625 --> 00:04:11,750
The merge that happens
from git will align

104
00:04:11,750 --> 00:04:13,760
these histories for us and merge

105
00:04:13,760 --> 00:04:16,700
the changes inappropriately
on the master branch.

106
00:04:16,700 --> 00:04:18,905
But one downside is that

107
00:04:18,905 --> 00:04:20,510
this commit then hasn't

108
00:04:20,510 --> 00:04:22,760
really been tested
with this commit.

109
00:04:22,760 --> 00:04:25,445
Git can combine
those two together,

110
00:04:25,445 --> 00:04:28,280
but you won't really be sure
that this change didn't

111
00:04:28,280 --> 00:04:29,330
break something that wasn't

112
00:04:29,330 --> 00:04:31,780
this commit unless you
specifically test it.

113
00:04:31,780 --> 00:04:33,770
One way you can
avoid this scenario

114
00:04:33,770 --> 00:04:36,070
is to use what's
called git-rebase.

115
00:04:36,070 --> 00:04:37,925
If you git rebase,

116
00:04:37,925 --> 00:04:39,530
what you're doing
is you're taking

117
00:04:39,530 --> 00:04:42,320
the feature branch that you
did originally develop off

118
00:04:42,320 --> 00:04:44,210
this commit and you're moving it

119
00:04:44,210 --> 00:04:46,960
back onto a later
commit from the master.

120
00:04:46,960 --> 00:04:49,100
You're essentially
replaying each of

121
00:04:49,100 --> 00:04:52,060
these commits on a new
commit from master.

122
00:04:52,060 --> 00:04:54,710
That would mean that
this commit here,

123
00:04:54,710 --> 00:04:56,255
rather than being based on

124
00:04:56,255 --> 00:04:58,655
the original commit for master,

125
00:04:58,655 --> 00:05:02,050
it would then be based on this
commit for master instead.

126
00:05:02,050 --> 00:05:05,330
The drawback potentially of

127
00:05:05,330 --> 00:05:08,120
doing this change is that
when you're going to rewrite

128
00:05:08,120 --> 00:05:09,530
the history of this branch

129
00:05:09,530 --> 00:05:11,120
because you're going
to be basing it off on

130
00:05:11,120 --> 00:05:12,320
a different commit than what you

131
00:05:12,320 --> 00:05:14,210
originally based it off of.

132
00:05:14,210 --> 00:05:16,655
If you're sharing work with
someone else on the branch,

133
00:05:16,655 --> 00:05:19,340
it would mean that they'd
need to force update,

134
00:05:19,340 --> 00:05:21,935
to match the changes
in the branch.

135
00:05:21,935 --> 00:05:23,795
You just need again,

136
00:05:23,795 --> 00:05:25,325
a policy with how to deal

137
00:05:25,325 --> 00:05:27,220
with this based on
your development team

138
00:05:27,220 --> 00:05:28,880
and a common strategy that

139
00:05:28,880 --> 00:05:30,725
everybody uses for
handling this scenario,

140
00:05:30,725 --> 00:05:32,850
but either one can work.

141
00:05:34,850 --> 00:05:41,180
Talking specifically about
another git workflow

142
00:05:41,180 --> 00:05:42,980
that's based on feature branch

143
00:05:42,980 --> 00:05:46,385
that you might hear
about is called GitFlow.

144
00:05:46,385 --> 00:05:48,170
GitFlow again is based on

145
00:05:48,170 --> 00:05:50,570
the basic Git feature
branch workflow where

146
00:05:50,570 --> 00:05:54,980
we move development of features
onto a specific branch,

147
00:05:54,980 --> 00:05:56,800
but it also moves,

148
00:05:56,800 --> 00:06:00,005
in general, all development
onto a development branch.

149
00:06:00,005 --> 00:06:01,880
The master branch,
rather than being

150
00:06:01,880 --> 00:06:03,814
the target of the
mergers of features,

151
00:06:03,814 --> 00:06:06,710
the master branch really
only tracks release history.

152
00:06:06,710 --> 00:06:08,360
The only time you're changing

153
00:06:08,360 --> 00:06:09,920
the master branch is
when you're building

154
00:06:09,920 --> 00:06:14,130
a new release for customers
or for release to QA.

155
00:06:14,130 --> 00:06:16,070
The development branch is always

156
00:06:16,070 --> 00:06:17,540
used for the integration of

157
00:06:17,540 --> 00:06:18,920
features before you're ready to

158
00:06:18,920 --> 00:06:21,785
do a release to customers or QA.

159
00:06:21,785 --> 00:06:24,200
You can look at the definition

160
00:06:24,200 --> 00:06:25,970
of the different
branches like this,

161
00:06:25,970 --> 00:06:29,060
where the master is
in blue here and

162
00:06:29,060 --> 00:06:32,870
the release branch
here is in this light,

163
00:06:32,870 --> 00:06:36,485
bluish-green and the
develop here is in purple.

164
00:06:36,485 --> 00:06:39,185
Then each one of the feature
branches, are in green.

165
00:06:39,185 --> 00:06:41,150
You can see that in these case

166
00:06:41,150 --> 00:06:43,670
we've started new branches
to work on features.

167
00:06:43,670 --> 00:06:44,810
In this case, we've merged

168
00:06:44,810 --> 00:06:47,165
the feature back into
the development branch.

169
00:06:47,165 --> 00:06:50,455
Here's where we decide we're
ready to do a release build.

170
00:06:50,455 --> 00:06:52,490
We might have done
one release build to

171
00:06:52,490 --> 00:06:55,190
internal QA that
failed and tried to do

172
00:06:55,190 --> 00:06:57,140
a new release build with
some additional patches

173
00:06:57,140 --> 00:06:59,980
here and ultimately done
or released to customers.

174
00:06:59,980 --> 00:07:02,440
It's only after we
do that release to

175
00:07:02,440 --> 00:07:03,830
customers that we actually do

176
00:07:03,830 --> 00:07:06,590
the merge into the
master branch.

177
00:07:06,590 --> 00:07:10,190
One nice thing that this allows

178
00:07:10,190 --> 00:07:13,055
us to do when we base
on the GitFlow workflow

179
00:07:13,055 --> 00:07:15,230
like this is we can have

180
00:07:15,230 --> 00:07:16,790
a separate parallel set of

181
00:07:16,790 --> 00:07:19,145
development that's happening
on our develop branch.

182
00:07:19,145 --> 00:07:20,540
Then maybe we find out there was

183
00:07:20,540 --> 00:07:21,800
a critical bug with one of

184
00:07:21,800 --> 00:07:24,590
the releases that we did
in our master branch.

185
00:07:24,590 --> 00:07:27,950
Well, what we can do then is
create a branch off master,

186
00:07:27,950 --> 00:07:29,710
that fixes that
one critical bug,

187
00:07:29,710 --> 00:07:33,105
do another release
of version 0.2,

188
00:07:33,105 --> 00:07:35,190
merge that back into
the development branch,

189
00:07:35,190 --> 00:07:36,810
and have not interrupted

190
00:07:36,810 --> 00:07:38,120
any of the development
work that we've

191
00:07:38,120 --> 00:07:41,510
done in process on our
development branch.

192
00:07:41,510 --> 00:07:43,460
It's just a way to

193
00:07:43,460 --> 00:07:47,120
make an easy place that everybody
can go to to see what's

194
00:07:47,120 --> 00:07:49,715
been released to
customers and handle

195
00:07:49,715 --> 00:07:51,920
this rather common scenario

196
00:07:51,920 --> 00:07:54,840
of needing to do
a hot fix match.